text:
I'd just consider her a friend at this point. Relationships recovering from this stuff is very rare. Move on. At least the breakup wasn't nasty. You guys will be able to salvage a friendship or she'll realize that this was a huge mistake(don't count on it).

&#x200B;

I'd advise you not to be overly present in her life for a bit after this. People really like being able to have their cake and eat it too during these breaks. They'll break up with you so they can "do them" but still expect the same level of emotional support you provided as a significant other, and that's just not fair on it's face. Most of the time people doing this aren't doing this because they need time to explore themselves, they are doing it because the relationship is no longer working or there is another person in the picture and they don't want to feel like an asshole.

&#x200B;

Good luck, from someone who's been on both ends of this scenario.

What would you do if someone else told you the same story? That shit is fucked, the girl went on a date as soon as you guys became official and decided the guy she went on a date with was a better choice. Now she thinks you might have been the better choice so she wants to leave him for you... What do you think will happen if you take her back and then she meets a new guy that she thinks is an even better choice? Same shit all over again

&#x200B;

Waste of your time, the fact that she isn't even single yet should be proof of that to you

&#x200B;
